      "interactions": [
        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 149.65,
            "to": 159.65
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>After axillary surgery, a patient has weakness in flexing the arm at the shoulde</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>After axillary surgery, a patient has weakness in flexing the arm at the shoulder. Examination reveals wasting of the clavicular head of pectoralis major, while the sternocostal head appears intact. Which nerve is most likely injured?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Long thoracic nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior. Its injury causes winging of the scapula, not selective wasting of the clavicular head of pectoralis major.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Medial pectoral nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The medial pectoral nerve (C8, T1) supplies the sternocostal head. Since the sternocostal head is intact in this patient, this nerve is not the one injured.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lateral pectoral nerve</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The lateral pectoral nerve (C5, 6, 7) supplies the clavicular head of pectoralis major, which is specifically responsible for flexion of the arm at the shoulder.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thoracodorsal nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The thoracodorsal nerve supplies latissimus dorsi. Its injury causes weakness in extension, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm, not selective pectoralis major wasting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Pectoralis major has two functionally distinct heads with separate nerve supplies. The clavicular head (lateral pectoral nerve, C5–7) flexes the arm, while the sternocostal head (medial pectoral nerve, C8–T1) extends from a flexed position. Selective wasting points to the nerve supplying that specific head."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The lateral pectoral nerve (C5, 6, 7) exclusively supplies the clavicular head of pectoralis major. The clavicular head flexes the arm forward up to 90 degrees — a distinct action from the sternocostal head's role in extension from a flexed position."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
