        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 311.975,
            "to": 321.975
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During the insertion of a central venous catheter via the cephalic vein, through</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During the insertion of a central venous catheter via the cephalic vein, through which fascial layer does the vein pass to join the axillary vein?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pectoral fascia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The pectoral fascia invests pectoralis major superficially. The cephalic vein passes deeper, through the clavipectoral fascia, to reach the axillary vein.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deep cervical fascia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The deep cervical fascia is located in the neck. The cephalic vein joins the axillary vein at the deltopectoral triangle by traversing the clavipectoral fascia, not the cervical fascia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Axillary fascia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The axillary fascia forms the floor of the axilla. The cephalic vein enters the axillary vein by piercing the clavipectoral fascia, not the axillary fascia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clavipectoral fascia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The cephalic vein is one of four structures that pierce the clavipectoral fascia, remembered by the mnemonic LCTA: Lateral pectoral nerve, Cephalic vein, Thoracoacromial artery, and Axillary lymphatics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Four structures pierce the clavipectoral fascia (mnemonic LCTA): Lateral pectoral nerve, Cephalic vein, Thoracoacromial artery, and Axillary lymphatics. The cephalic vein's passage through this fascia makes it a clinically important landmark for venous access."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The cephalic vein pierces the clavipectoral fascia to drain into the axillary vein. This is why the deltopectoral groove, where the vein is most accessible, is used for central venous catheter insertion."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
