          "label": "<p>A 50-year-old woman presents with a hard breast lump. On examination, the overly</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 50-year-old woman presents with a hard breast lump. On examination, the overlying skin is pulled inward, producing dimpling. Which breast structure, when infiltrated by tumour, causes this sign?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cooper's ligaments</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Cooper's ligaments (suspensory ligaments of the breast) are fibrous strands running from the skin to the deep fascia. Tumour infiltration causes them to shorten, pulling the overlying skin inward — the classic dimple sign of breast malignancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lactiferous sinuses</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lactiferous sinuses are milk reservoirs near the nipple. Their involvement by tumour may cause nipple discharge or retraction, but skin dimpling is specifically caused by Cooper's ligament infiltration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Retromammary space</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The retromammary space contains loose areolar tissue between the breast and deep fascia. Tumour invasion here causes fixation of the breast to the chest wall, not skin dimpling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lobular alveoli</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lobular alveoli are the milk-secreting units of the breast. While lobular carcinoma arises from these cells, skin dimpling is caused by infiltration and shortening of Cooper's ligaments, not the alveoli themselves.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Skin dimpling is a classic sign of breast malignancy. It results specifically from tumour infiltration of Cooper's ligaments (the suspensory ligaments of the breast), which are fibrous strands connecting the skin to the deep fascia. When shortened by tumour, they retract the overlying skin."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Cooper's ligaments subdivide the breast and anchor it between the skin and the deep fascia. When a malignant tumour infiltrates and shortens these ligaments, the overlying skin is pulled inward — producing the dimple sign described in the opening case."
                }
              ],
